Scenario
You are rapidly prototyping a Gen AI agent that creates real estate investment opportunity reports on the development potential of vacant lots. You are working with a team of real estate analysts who have helped you ideate some rubric criteria to evaluate the reports, but they have not provided the labels necessary to perform human/LLM judge agreement studies. Because you are working at speed, you are performing consistency studies on the criteria to determine what criteria might be suitable for autorating. You plan to do a few hill climbing iterations, as needed, based on the rubric criteria that might be reliable.

You prototyped the agent with Agent Development Kit (ADK) and powered it with Gemini, tools you are very familiar with, so you can work efficiently. The design of the agent is intentionally unsophisticated—it primarily is based on skills that an analyst helped you quickly draft to help you get started.

Your challenge
You must accomplish the following phases of agent evaluation and hill climbing:

Generate agent responses: Execute the re_analyst agent across a batch of target property IDs to generate a baseline dataset of investment reports.

Evaluate with LLM-as-a-Judge: Use the Gen AI evaluation service on Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform to score the agent's reports against a predefined rubric.

Analyze evaluation consistency: Because LLM judges can be inconsistent, you must run multi-pass evaluations (k-passes) and analyze the consistency of ratings using inter-/intra-rater reliability metrics to identify which rubric criteria are potentially reliable.

Improve autorater scores via hill climbing: Iteratively refine the agent's skills until it achieves a target mean evaluation score of above 4.0 on the target criteria.

