Task 3. Generate agent responses
In this task, you create an evaluation dataset by executing the real estate investment opportunity analysis agent across target vacant lot IDs. Evaluation harness code provided with the lab automates this for you.

Open a new Cloud Shell terminal, navigate to the autoraters directory, set the Python version to 3.13, and ensure your environment is activated by running the following commands:

cd ~/autoraters
source ~/.bashrc
pyenv global 3.13
gcloud config set project qwiklabs-gcp-01-282bb1a3e420
Copied!
Run the evaluation dataset builder. This script reads target property Lot IDs from eval_harness/hillclimb_lots.txt, executes the re_analyst agent across all 10 properties to record agent telemetry, and saves the fully populated evaluation dataset to ./re_analyst/hillclimb_lots.evalset.json:

python3 -m eval_harness.create_re_agent_evalset --lots-file eval_harness/hillclimb_lots.txt --out ./re_analyst/hillclimb_lots.evalset.json --agent-dir ./re_analyst
Copied!
In your open ADK Web UI tab (http://127.0.0.1:8000), select re_analyst from the top-left dropdown, click Eval History (or History), click the refresh button, and select hillclimb_lots to explore the step-by-step tool invocations and generated evaluation reports.

Note: If Gemini capacity is limited not all agent executions will complete, but you can still proceed with the lab as long as a few reports were successfully generated.
Task 4. Evaluate the agent responses with LLM-as-a-Judge
In this task, you use the Gen AI evaluation service on Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform to evaluate the quality of the generated real estate investment opportunity analysis reports.

You run an evaluation runner script (run_geap_eval.py) that evaluates your agent's generated reports against the rubric defined in eval_harness/rubrics.json using Gemini as a judge. The script calls the Gen AI evaluation service k times to collect multiple scores and explanations for each report, which helps analyze the consistency of the LLM judge.

Step 1: Perform a dry run to inspect the evaluator prompts
Before making live API calls to Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform, run the script in dry-run mode. This parses your criteria and reports, assembles the exact prompts sent to the LLM judge, and saves everything locally without invoking the cloud APIs.

Run the following command in your terminal:

python3 -m eval_harness.run_geap_eval \
  --traces re_analyst/hillclimb_lots.evalset.json \
  --rubric-file eval_harness/rubrics.json \
  --out runs/baseline/geap_calls_and_responses.json \
  --experiment-name rubric-agreement-check \
  --dry-run \
  --k 3
Copied!
Inspect the generated file runs/baseline/geap_calls_and_responses.json to see the following:

prompt_made_to_service—The fully rendered prompt containing the instructions, evaluation criteria, rating scale, and the agent's report.
is_dry_run—A boolean flag set to true, indicating that this record is a dry run and does not contain live responses.
Step 2: Execute live cloud evaluations
Now, run the script without the --dry-run flag to dispatch the evaluation requests to the Gen AI evaluation service. You run 3 passes (--k 3) for each case to gather multiple ratings per report.

Execute the live evaluation runner using the following command:

python3 -m eval_harness.run_geap_eval \
  --traces re_analyst/hillclimb_lots.evalset.json \
  --rubric-file eval_harness/rubrics.json \
  --out runs/baseline/geap_calls_and_responses.json \
  --experiment-name rubric-agreement-check \
  --k 3
Copied!
Note: This makes real API calls to the Gen AI evaluation service on Agent Platform using the Google Cloud SDK. If you run into timeouts, 429 errors, or other capacity-related errors you can still proceed with the lab. Subsequent tasks do not depend on the result of this task, beyond the inspection of the results in the remaining steps of this task.
The Gen AI evaluation service uses majority voting of four votes when scoring rubrics. When doing agreement analysis later this is important to remember, since it artificially inflates agreement metrics.

Once completed, inspect runs/baseline/geap_calls_and_responses.json. Under responses_from_service for each record, you should see the live model-based evaluation scores and detailed textual explanations returned by the Gemini judge model.

In Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform Experiments in the Cloud console, open the experiment, then open the individual runs to view the metrics for the different rubric criteria.

Task 5. Analyze autorater intra-rater reliability
The evaluation you just ran was intentionally small, too small for meaningful autorater intra-rater reliability analysis. Rather than force you to run a large enough job and wait a long time, the results of a larger run have already been created in the file runs/geap_calls_and_responses_for_analysis.json. This is 32 lot IDs graded on 19 criteria across 15 repeated evaluation passes.

In this task, you use this data to analyze the rubric and determine which criteria are not being used consistently by the autoraters.

Step 1: Perform a deep-dive into an individual criterion's autorater scores
Use the single_criterion_analysis.py tool to view the detailed score matrix of lot IDs (N) vs. evaluation passes (k) for a single criterion:

cd ~/autoraters
python3 -m eval_harness.single_criterion_analysis \
  --run_geap_eval_out runs/geap_calls_and_responses_for_analysis.json \
  --criterion environmental-esa
Copied!
Tip: If you are having problems with analysis Python scripts that print tabular output to the terminal, remember that you can pipe the output of any terminal command to a text file by adding > outputfile.txt at the end of the command. You can then open the text file in the Cloud Shell Editor.
Step 2: Review metrics for one criterion
The single_criterion_analysis.py tool provides the following metrics about criterion autorater quality. Be careful: some metrics are distractors and do not provide useful info for analyzing autorater quality!

Table metrics summary
Mean Rating (mean): The average of all scores (all passes, all lot IDs).
Min All Ratings (min Rating): The lowest score assigned by any judge pass on any Lot ID.
Max All Ratings (Max Rating): The highest score assigned by any judge pass on any Lot ID.
Widest Range Single Report (Max Range Report): The largest score difference (Max - Min) observed for a single lot ID's report across the evaluation passes. It also highlights the specific Lot ID. A large range (e.g., 2 or 3) shows severe disagreement between the eval passes on that lot ID's report.
Widest Range Single Pass (Max Range Pass): The largest score difference (Max - Min) observed for a single eval pass across the reports for all Lot IDs.
StdDev of Report Means (SD Report Means): Take the mean score for each lot ID's report across the k evaluation passes. Then, calculate the standard deviation of these means. This indicates how much the perceived quality of reports varied.
StdDev of Pass Means (SD Pass Means): Take the mean score of each evaluation pass (across all the Lot ID reports). Then calculate the standard deviation of these means.
Mean Pairwise Percent Agreement of Report Ratings (Mean Pairwise Agree): The average pairwise percent agreement of all reports. For a single report, pairwise percent agreement measures the percentage of rater pairs that gave the exact same score out of all possible rater pairs.
Krippendorff's Alpha (Alpha): A global reliability coefficient measuring agreement collectively across all reports and passes.
Margins of the grades matrix:
Right margin columns (min, max, range, mean, std, %_pw_ag) allow you to inspect the scoring variance and agreement for each report. %_pw_ag is percent pairwise agreement: if all pairs of ratings (ignoring missing ratings) are compared, this is the percentage of paired ratings that are identical.
Bottom margin rows (pass mean, pass std, pass min, pass max, pass range) let you check the behavior of each evaluation pass.
Step 3: View metrics for all criteria
To help speed up your analysis, use all_criteria_analysis.py to view the top table metrics from single_criterion_analysis.py across all 19 criteria simultaneously.

Run the following command:

cd ~/autoraters
python3 -m eval_harness.all_criteria_analysis \
  --run_geap_eval_out runs/geap_calls_and_responses_for_analysis.json
Copied!
Step 4: Choose reliable autorater criteria
Use single_criterion_analysis.py and all_criteria_analysis.py to identify which autorater criteria show the best intra-rater agreement.

Open the file best_criteria_submission.txt in the Cloud Shell editor, and write the criterion-ids of 3 criteria you think show the best intra-rater reliability. The criterion_ids are listed in the first column (criterion_id) of the output of all_criteria_analysis.py, for example analyst-insight.

Separate the criterion-ids by commas. For example, if the criteria with criterion_ids "general-tone", "data-density", and "analyst-insight" turn out to be the best to use, best_criteria_submission.txt should look like this:
general-tone,data-density,analyst-insight
Copied!
Next, upload that file to Cloud Storage with the following command. When the upload is complete, check if you chose good criteria. There are more than 3 criteria that are potentially okay to use, but you only need to choose 3:
gcloud storage cp best_criteria_submission.txt gs://qwiklabs-gcp-01-282bb1a3e420-bucket/best_criteria_submission.txt
Copied!
Tip: Remember, not all of the metrics are useful for assessing autorater criterion reliability.
Tip: Watch out for the homogeneity trap.
Tip: Remember, the backend majority vote of 4 means agreement metrics are slightly inflated, meaning the general guidelines for interpreting alpha might be a bit lenient. Be conservative in what you consider acceptable agreement.
Click Check my progress to verify the objective.
AT ID: 1 Choose reliable criteria

Task 6. Hill climb agent skills to raise autorater criteria scores
Now that you have identified some autorater criteria which might be suitable to use, your next task is to modify the agent to improve its performance.

The only properties you want to optimize and evaluate during this phase are listed in eval_harness/hillclimb_lots.txt.

While you wish you could perform human agreement analysis first with the criteria that show high intra-rater agreement across multiple autorater runs, the tight deadline for this prototype means you do not have time.

Step 1: First hill climbing run
To speed up hill climbing iteration cycles, an automated script run_hillclimb.py is provided. This script executes your active agent in the re_analyst directory across the target lots, dispatches a single-pass (k=1) evaluation call to the Gen AI evaluation service, and calculates the mean score for two target criteria:

cost-per-acre (Acquisition Cost-Per-Acre Calculation)
neighboring-flood-risk (Neighboring Property Flood Risk Analysis)
While you have concerns about the questionable intra-rater agreement you saw in the earlier analysis for one of these metrics, your project sponsors are adamant that the problem this criterion detects must be fixed. Thankfully, the agreement was moderate. You'll be sure to note your concern in your final report.

Run the script now to see the scores of your current agent:
cd ~/autoraters
python3 -m eval_harness.run_hillclimb
Copied!
Each run of the script automatically increments and saves the agent traces to a versioned file at re_analyst/hillclimb_X.evalset.json and the results of the Gen AI evaluation service to a versioned folder under runs/hillclimb_X/.

Note: You may see warnings about skipping cases for evaluation if some Gemini calls time out. It's not a problem if some of the cases don't complete, and the code will proceed to evaluate the cases that do complete.
Step 2: Analyze the hill climbing results
When the hill climbing run is complete, the mean score for each of the autorated criteria prints to the terminal. They should be well below the target means of above 4.0.

Inspect the Gemini judge's detailed grading rationales using the explain_criteria_results.py tool:
cd ~/autoraters
python3 -m eval_harness.explain_criteria_results \
   --run_geap_eval_out runs/hillclimb_1/geap_calls_and_responses_hillclimb_1.json \
   --criterion cost-per-acre \
   --max_score_to_show 4.0
Copied!
As you hillclimb, change --criterion to examine explanations for another criterion and --run_geap_eval_out to view the results of future eval runs by incrementing the number at the end of the filename.

--max_score_to_show only shows explanations for Lot IDs with scores equal to or below this value. The --max_score_to_show default of 4.0 means Lot IDs where the report scored 5 do not have their explanation printed.

You need to look at three sources of information to understand your agent's deficiencies:

The output of explain_criteria_results.py.
The criterion definitions in eval_harness/rubrics.json.
The agent runs in the evalsets, which you can view in adk web in the /autoraters/re_analyst space.
Use this tool, along with viewing the description of the criteria in eval_harness/rubrics.json to understand where your agent is deficient.

Step 3: Improve the agent and iterate
Modify the re_analyst's skills. When you think you have improved them, run python3 -m eval_harness.run_hillclimb again. Repeat until your mean score on both criteria is above 4.0.

Important: The Check my progress button below only checks the last evaluation run. As soon as both scores are above 4.0 in one run, make sure to click the button. Otherwise, if you keep experimenting for fun and accidentally go below 4.0, you may not get credit for prior improvements.
Note: If Gemini capacity issues are slowing you down, you can set the agent to run using gemini-3.1-flash-lite by changing .env. It is still possible to complete this lab with an agent that runs with flash-lite.
Click Check my progress to verify the objective.
AT ID: 2 Perform hill-climbing automation

If you have time remaining in the lab, the lab authors recommend that you review the code in run_geap_eval.py to enhance your learning. This code extracts responses from ADK traces and packages them up with custom rubrics in rubrics.json for evaluation in the Gen AI evaluation service on Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform.


